1
00:00:00,570 --> 00:00:03,488
Look closely at the Statue of Liberty's feet.

2
00:00:03,868 --> 00:00:10,311
Her right heel is off the ground. She isn't standing still. She's mid-stride.

3
00:00:10,613 --> 00:00:16,831
She has been since 1886. So what if, one night, she finished the step?

4
00:00:17,392 --> 00:00:20,221
DEE: Ma'am? Ma'am, you can't... Okay.

5
00:00:21,591 --> 00:00:26,922
Home is Paris. She was built there, from copper sheets about as thick as two pennies.

6
00:00:26,964 --> 00:00:31,830
In 1884 she stood finished above the Paris rooftops.

7
00:00:31,912 --> 00:00:36,861
Then she was taken apart, packed into 214 crates, and shipped to New York.

8
00:00:36,958 --> 00:00:40,552
Her iron skeleton was engineered by Gustave Eiffel.

9
00:00:40,590 --> 00:00:43,570
A few years later, he built something taller.

10
00:00:44,620 --> 00:00:49,197
By sunrise, she's wading across New York Harbor, and the whole city is filming.

11
00:00:49,483 --> 00:00:51,441
TONY: I literally just bought the T-shirt!

12
00:00:51,485 --> 00:00:54,962
The harbour seals dive. The gulls take off.

13
00:00:55,047 --> 00:00:58,965
The Staten Island Ferry reverses, very, very carefully.

14
00:00:59,414 --> 00:01:03,500
Where's she going? East. Home.

15
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:08,660
Paris is about five thousand eight hundred kilometres away.

16
00:01:08,735 --> 00:01:12,753
Something her size would stroll at about twenty-four kilometres an hour.

17
00:01:12,773 --> 00:01:17,213
That's ten days, nonstop. Statues don't need sleep.

18
00:01:18,263 --> 00:01:21,581
Past the harbour mouth, the sea floor drops away.

19
00:01:21,714 --> 00:01:24,881
The water closes over her crown, then her torch.

20
00:01:24,937 --> 00:01:28,675
New York's humpback whales have been coming back in big numbers.

21
00:01:28,767 --> 00:01:30,921
None of them expected this.

22
00:01:32,263 --> 00:01:39,091
Day four. Three thousand eight hundred metres down. Pitch black, except for her torch.

23
00:01:39,309 --> 00:01:43,700
The pressure here is nearly four hundred times what it is at the surface.

24
00:01:43,848 --> 00:01:48,691
But she's hollow, and full of seawater, so it pushes back from inside just as hard.

25
00:01:49,029 --> 00:01:52,423
And then, in the torchlight, a shape everyone knows.

26
00:01:53,104 --> 00:01:57,930
She lowers her torch for a moment. Then she keeps walking.

27
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:04,536
Day six. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge, part of the longest mountain range on Earth.

28
00:02:04,628 --> 00:02:07,163
Almost all of it is underwater.

29
00:02:07,183 --> 00:02:12,983
She climbs it in the dark. On the far side, the sea floor starts rising towards Europe.

30
00:02:14,113 --> 00:02:17,777
Day ten. Sunrise in Normandy.

31
00:02:18,007 --> 00:02:21,543
The tides at Mont-Saint-Michel are among the biggest in Europe,

32
00:02:21,563 --> 00:02:23,869
up to about fourteen metres.

33
00:02:23,919 --> 00:02:27,303
The salt-meadow sheep are used to water coming in fast.

34
00:02:27,678 --> 00:02:29,503
They are not used to this.

35
00:02:31,033 --> 00:02:36,598
Paris. The Arc de Triomphe is fifty metres tall. She's forty-six.

36
00:02:36,663 --> 00:02:41,511
In New York, she has a pedestal. Here, she has to stand on her toes.

37
00:02:43,453 --> 00:02:50,274
Parisians last saw her in 1884, the colour of a new penny. She's come back green.

38
00:02:50,371 --> 00:02:56,303
And her skeleton and that tower came from the same engineer. Time for a family photo.

39
00:02:57,373 --> 00:03:01,342
Except the Eiffel Tower is three hundred and thirty metres tall.

40
00:03:02,044 --> 00:03:04,548
She doesn't even reach the first floor.

41
00:03:06,613 --> 00:03:10,365
And on a narrow island in the Seine, someone has been waiting.

42
00:03:10,716 --> 00:03:14,944
A quarter-size copy of her, put up in 1889.

43
00:03:14,964 --> 00:03:19,211
Since 1937, she has faced west, towards New York.

44
00:03:19,608 --> 00:03:25,732
Eighty-nine years of looking out to sea. And today, her big sister walked out of it.

45
00:03:27,033 --> 00:03:29,933
Back in New York, the pedestal is empty.

46
00:03:30,331 --> 00:03:32,067
TONY: So... do I get a refund?

47
00:03:32,317 --> 00:03:34,289
DEE: She'll be back. Probably.

48
00:03:35,533 --> 00:03:38,125
And she may not be the only wanderer.

49
00:03:38,203 --> 00:03:42,929
Some archaeologists think Easter Island's statues were walked into place,

50
00:03:43,012 --> 00:03:45,043
rocked upright with ropes.

51
00:03:45,499 --> 00:03:49,933
So which landmark should walk next? Tell me in the comments.
